WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.171
John: Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.171 --> 00:00:02.780
I'm going to try an experiment today.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.240
I have to make a video today, what should
I make it about?

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.090
Henry: A velociraptor!

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.330
John: Velociraptors?

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.330
I'm into it.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.460
Okay, the velociraptor.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:16.360
So, the velociraptor was not a famous dinosaur
until Michael Crichton's 1990 novel Jurassic

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.360
Park.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.620
In that book, and the subsequent Steven Spielberg
movie, a velociraptor skeleton is excavated

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.370
in Montana, and there are these, like, terrifying
creatures.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:27.740
They're smart and almost people-sized, and
they've got those weird reptilian scales and

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.880
they hunt in packs taking down dinos and people
alike.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.040
And even if you haven't seen Jurassic Park,
and God knows Henry hasn't, you're still familiar

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.820
with, like, the terror of the velociraptor,
right?

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.482
Like, Toronto named its NBA team after the
raptors, because they're just... ohh, you

00:00:42.482 --> 00:00:45.290
wanna stay away from them, they're gonna beat
you at basketball!

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:49.790
Yeah, except 1) The velociraptor never actually
lived in Montana, it lived in Mongolia in

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:54.160
China, 2) Velociraptors were not scaly, they
were feathered, 3) They probably didn't hunt

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.460
in packs, 4) They could not have hung out
in Jurassic Park, because they lived 75 million

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.650
years ago, in the Late Cretaceous Period,
and 5) They were not human-sized killing machines,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.680
they were, like, long-tailed turkeys.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.180
In short, Hank, the Toronto Raptors are named
after a Mongolian creature that probably looked

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.180
like this.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.880
Adult velociraptors weighed less than the
average three-year-old human, and although

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.350
their name means "swift thief", in fact they
were probably quite slow, because they were

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.670
walking on short little turkey legs.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:27.491
Ohh, off-topic, but at the State Fair turkey
legs are just so delicious; I wonder if velociraptor

00:01:27.491 --> 00:01:28.491
legs are good...

00:01:28.491 --> 00:01:32.150
You may have noticed that in real life, there
is one animal species that's truly terrifying,

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:33.270
and it's us.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.390
We would have posed a far greater threat to
velociraptors than they would have posed to

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:38.390
us.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.619
Anyway, the velociraptors in Jurassic Park
are based off of a larger American cousin

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:42.619
called deinonychus.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:46.460
But they called them velociraptors because
that's a much cooler name, Hank!

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.850
And that actually happens a lot in the world
of dinousaurs.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:52.030
Like, Hank, when we were kids, the coolest
dinosaur in the world was the brontosaurus,

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.920
which means "thunder lizard".

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.450
But Hank, it turns out that brontosaurus isn't
even a thing, it's just an apatosaurus, which

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.290
means "deceptive lizard", which isn't nearly
as cool.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:05.290
I don't want my gigantic lizards to bring
the lies, I want them to bring the thunder.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.560
Also, as Henry will happily point out to you,
apatosaurus isn't even that big, certainly

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.970
not compared to diplodocus.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.290
So why did velociraptors have feathers?

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.970
Well, they were part of the bird-like Dromaeosaurid
family, all of which had feathers, and some

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.599
of which could probably actually fly.

00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:19.350
But velociraptors couldn't fly, and they may
have used their feathers to, like, attract

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:23.400
mates, but probably it's just an evolutionary
left-over, the way that Blind Mole-rats still

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.349
have eyes and Humpback Whales have vestigial
hind legs.

00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:29.980
But now that I've disappointed you by telling
you the truth about velociraptors I'm gonna

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:31.950
tell you two awesome things about them.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.590
First, they could jump, like, ten feet high,
vertical.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.520
Secondly, they had these retractable sickle-shaped
claws in their hind legs that they used to,

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.940
like, stab their prey.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.840
And we know they could kill the somewhat larger,
although not as large as you would expect,

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.840
protoceratops.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.430
We know this partly because there's a fossil
of a velociraptor and a protoceratops locked

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:50.510
in combat.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.750
And then while they were fighting a sandstorm
came and drowned them in sand.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:57.930
Hank, the pleasure of having a dinosaur-obsessed
child is that you realize both how much we

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.170
know and how much we don't know about the
distant past of life on Earth.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.870
Like, Hank, the reason that Micheal Criton's
velociraptors were featherless is that we

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:11.130
didn't know for sure until 2007 that velociraptors
were feathered because that's when scientists

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.540
discovered quill knobs on one of their forearms.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.769
There are a lot of cool things about dinosaurs,
Hank, but the coolest thing is that 100 million

00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:20.920
years later we're still learning.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.360
Thanks to Henry for the video topic.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:23.549
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

